WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
- For those of you who don't know,

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.240
this is Roxy, my Husky.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.550
She's the OG rockstar that helped me

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.680
launch my social media career.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:08.820
- [Newscaster] He has more than 2 million

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.260
followers on Instagram.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.183
Talk about a fan club.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.400
- Today I wanna share
a very important story

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.440
of something that happened with Roxy.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:20.670
It's a terrible situation.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.070
As you can tell, she's now a tripod,

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.950
a three legged dog, and she's doing well

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.260
but this situation
could have been avoided.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.160
And I wanna arm you

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.080
with information that
you should know when you

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.030
have to make a choice
when your pet gets sick.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:33.863
Roxy come.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.410
Sit. Good dog.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:40.470
Good doggie. You good dog.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:44.160
So to set up the story,
Roxy now lives with my dad.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.920
So he takes care of her vet stuff,

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.360
takes her in for checkups.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:52.889
And Roxy had sort of a bump
growing on her right arm.

00:00:52.889 --> 00:00:56.250
And this bump was there for several years.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.130
It may have grown slightly
over the course of those years,

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:00.750
but this bump never bothered her.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.000
So I wasn't too concerned about it

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.170
but my dad said, "You know what?

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.050
I'm gonna point it out to the
vet on the upcoming visit",

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:08.550
which I thought was a very good idea.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.830
So when they went to go
get this bump checked

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.510
out and an overall physical,
they said, you know,

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.630
for 12 years old, this
dog is doing very well,

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.970
in good health running all the time.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:18.810
She's so fit.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:19.920
She's so active.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.990
She looks like an eight
year old dog, but they said

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:23.250
we want to take a sample

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.790
of the bump on her leg
and send it for cytology,

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.250
which means that under a
microscope, they look at

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.620
and see what cells are there to decide

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.410
is this a cancerous lump,
Non-cancerous lump, et cetera.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.030
So after my dad went,

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.610
he gave my phone number
for the vet to call back.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.554
And then when the vet
ended up calling me back,

00:01:41.554 --> 00:01:44.340
they said "That unfortunately
the cytology came back

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.680
and it looks like this
is a cancerous bump."

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.330
They called it a sarcoma

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:50.940
which means that it's
a soft tissue cancer.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.457
And they recommended
bringing the dog back in

00:01:53.457 --> 00:01:56.386
for a consultation with the surgeon.

00:01:56.386 --> 00:02:00.750
I know how surgery is difficult on humans.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:02.400
And I could only imagine
how difficult it is

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.390
on dogs who need activity
in order to stay fit,

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:10.590
in order to stay mentally well,
especially dogs like Roxy.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:11.490
She's so active.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:12.480
She's a Husky.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.370
She has so much energy in her.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.468
So I told my dad a list

00:02:16.468 --> 00:02:20.670
of questions that I wanted
him to ask the surgeon.

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.280
And those questions are questions
that you should always ask

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:24.930
before going up for a procedure.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:29.460
A, is this type of cancer that
Roxy has dangerous in that

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.110
is it likely to spread?

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.020
Is it likely to cause problems
elsewhere in her body?

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.120
What's the five year survival rate?

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.110
What impact will this have on
her life as a 12 year old dog?

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:44.460
Is it worth for a 12 year
old Husky to go for a surgery

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:48.120
on her leg to remove a
tumor that so far has yet

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:48.990
to cause a problem?

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.300
And I thought my dad being a
physician would be well armed

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.640
to ask these questions and
I trusted him to do so.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.290
I actually don't talk

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.120
to him the following day
when he actually went

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:57.953
for the visit.

00:02:57.953 --> 00:02:58.820
And the next day he sends me

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.960
me a picture of Roxy
wearing a cast healing

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:03.750
up in the sun, in the backyard.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:04.620
And I thought, "Oh wow,

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.890
she must be doing so much
better because she's healing."

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:10.200
And he goes, "She's doing awesome post-op.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.000
The doctor told me that
it wouldn't be that big

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.908
of a procedure and that they
could likely do this, you know,

00:03:14.908 --> 00:03:17.640
in and out one day's time.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.350
And they thought it was beneficial."

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.750
I was a little bit skeptical,
but you know, seeing the fact

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.393
that she was recovering
was very promising to me.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:30.230
So over the next few days,
things started to change.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:33.840
She had to go in

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.080
for multiple wound checks
to get her wound examined

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.150
because the area where she had the tumor,

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:40.380
she had a very thick bandage on,

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.180
my dad couldn't check it himself.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.610
There were sutures in
place, staples in place

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:46.860
and they needed to constantly check this.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.180
So she was on antibiotics.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.410
They were doing these checks.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:50.790
I noticed that the bandage was

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:52.770
on really tight, but you know, I'm looking

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.906
at pictures and I'm trusting
my dad and all this stuff.

00:03:55.906 --> 00:03:59.052
And then like a week goes
by and my dad calls me

00:03:59.052 --> 00:04:00.967
while I'm working in
the hospital and says,

00:04:00.967 --> 00:04:02.820
"Mike, I need you to come home

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.620
after the hospital and look at Roxy's leg.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.360
I don't think it's good."

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.910
And I'm like, "But wait,
like, I'm not a surgeon, A.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:11.160
I'm not a vet B.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:11.993
If you think it's bad,

00:04:11.993 --> 00:04:14.040
that that means probably
we should go get it checked

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.150
out at the vet.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.950
And he was like, "No, I
have an appointment with him

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:18.780
like in two days, I'm gonna go then,

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:19.890
that's when the surgeon is there,

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.510
'cause he's on vacation."

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:22.440
You know, some complicated things,

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:23.640
so I said "Of course I'll come by."

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.210
So when I came by, I'm
looking at Roxy's foot,

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:30.810
it was now unwrapped and
this is a graphic photo

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:31.910
but I'm gonna show it.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.090
Her foot was dead.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:38.970
Her paw was dead. Like
there was no blood flow.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:40.410
It was a cold foot.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:41.670
It smelled gangrenous,

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.890
which means it was becoming infected.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:46.080
I was like, 'Dad, like, this is terrible.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.850
Like she's gonna lose her foot.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:51.150
Like there's no, like
this foot has clearly not

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:53.280
had circulation for a
long period of time."

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.070
And for me at what I wasn't
sure of was this an issue

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:59.310
of the bandage being very
tight or was this more

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:03.600
of an issue surgically that
when they removed the tumor

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:05.760
they also took out extra tissue

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.050
and possibly the blood vessels
that go down to her foot.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:11.610
And my dad like never clarified this

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:13.470
with the surgeon, Roxy leave it.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.020
So I was very like upset about that

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:19.290
and wondering the whole
time A, should have Roxy

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.140
have gotten the surgery, B,
did the surgeon make a mistake?

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:24.300
C, was the bandage on too tight?

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.850
And honestly, it's really hard to know

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:28.500
so that when my dad actually went in there

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:29.333
they basically said,

00:05:29.333 --> 00:05:31.230
"Yeah, look, we think we should amputate."

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.420
And my dad, let me speak to the surgeon.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.407
And I asked the surgeon,
very frankly, like,

00:05:35.407 --> 00:05:37.196
"Look, is this a mess up?

00:05:37.196 --> 00:05:39.783
Did we take out too much, you know,

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:43.200
tissue that the blood vessel
came out along with it?"

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.960
'Cause if you take out the arteries,

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.150
and the blood vessels
that are higher in the leg

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.120
they essentially feed circulation
to the bottom of the paw.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.190
It would never heal the
bottom of the paw then.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:55.080
Or was it that the
bandage was on too tight

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:57.930
and the rehab process,
or is she an older dog,

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:01.710
and this is a normal complication
that happens time to time?

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:03.807
And he basically said "All of the above."

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:09.037
And while I appreciate the
honesty, because he said,

00:06:09.037 --> 00:06:10.800
"Look, we had to take out a lot of tissue.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.137
The tumor was larger than we expected."

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:18.090
I think like if I was
working with a human surgeon,

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:22.140
I think that we would've
thought about those risks,

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:24.095
that if we open up,

00:06:24.095 --> 00:06:27.045
or are potentially getting
ready to open up a person's body

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:31.830
and then we see that we
can cause more damage

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:36.830
than actually benefit by doing
a biopsy or removing a tumor.

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:38.880
We actually don't do it.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.920
So I asked the surgeon,

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.640
like "What was the dog's
staging of the cancer?"

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.890
And it was a stage one sarcoma.

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:49.290
And not to say that this isn't serious,

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:53.040
it's just at a 12 years
old Roxy's life expectancy

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:55.200
is maybe another two years.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:56.340
She's getting in that range.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.890
It's sad to say obviously, but you know,

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:00.420
we're really hurting her quality of life

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.010
by putting her under the
stress of that surgery,

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:07.010
plus the risk of the things
going wrong, like right now.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.530
So poor Roxy in recovering
from the surgery was

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:13.770
barely able to walk, was
very sick during that time.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:17.160
And then now we're saying we
have to amputate her entire leg

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:20.220
and not just the entire
leg, the scapula as well,

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:22.500
because apparently dogs,
this is the way to do it.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.050
I asked about a prosthetic, but he said

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:28.290
that that would require a
second operation as well.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:30.030
And she would have to learn to use it

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:32.132
and an older dog and being a Husky

00:07:32.132 --> 00:07:35.700
which is a breed that tends
to be more picky with pain,

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.680
He said "That she might
not even adapt well to it."

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:41.400
He recommended just
taking off the entire leg

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:45.105
and kind of told me some new
information that I didn't know

00:07:45.105 --> 00:07:47.460
about dogs, that they tend
to handle amputations better

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:51.363
than humans, because they
don't have the psychological,

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:54.300
traumatic component of this,

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:57.990
where they don't miss
having the fourth leg.

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:59.610
That you know, she's like,
'Okay, I have three legs.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:00.443
This is what I do.

00:08:00.443 --> 00:08:01.276
And that's who I am.

00:08:01.276 --> 00:08:02.370
And that's the dog I am.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:03.570
And I move forward."

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:04.670
Can I get a high five?

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.597
Where's that messed up task.

00:08:07.597 --> 00:08:08.772
- Oh, good girl.

00:08:08.772 --> 00:08:10.039
- Wow
- Good girl.

00:08:10.039 --> 00:08:10.872
Here you get bonus.

00:08:10.872 --> 00:08:13.380
And because of that, they
seem to rebound quite well.

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:14.910
And I will say outta this whole process

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:17.250
that's probably been the
most accurate part of it,

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:19.140
that Roxy has recovered quite well.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:20.250
Thank God.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:21.300
She had a mean scar.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:24.720
You can't really see it now,
but it's healed up quite well.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:27.570
We took out the staples a few weeks ago.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:30.060
She's now going for
longer walks with my dad.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:32.550
When I come home, she's wagging her tail.

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:34.800
She's still shedding like
a monster as you can tell

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:38.040
from all of my clothes,
she's still super sweet.

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:41.130
She's happy, obviously
slightly lower energy

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:42.540
'cause she fatigues.

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:44.520
And now I know she has higher risks

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.990
of getting arthritis in her
legs because she's unbalanced.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:51.960
She has to put more stress
on each one of the joints.

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:53.010
Yeah. It's sad.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:54.330
It's sad watching, you know,

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:57.180
your dog gets sick with
whatever condition it is.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:00.120
So all in all this goes to say, you know

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:01.500
when you bring your dog to the vet,

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:04.260
make sure you're armed
with good information.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:06.240
Make sure you ask hard questions

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.000
of both not only the general
vet, but the surgeon.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.042
Make sure they give you
accurate risk assessments

00:09:11.042 --> 00:09:12.307
because it's very easy to say,

00:09:12.307 --> 00:09:13.440
"Oh, we'll just take this out.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:14.520
Easy peasy, no problem."

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.220
But then like your dog can lose a limb

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:18.526
and it could be quite frustrating

00:09:18.526 --> 00:09:20.970
because it feels like you
didn't get all the information

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:24.240
but know that pets are sadly not immortal.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:25.073
And you want to think

00:09:25.073 --> 00:09:28.140
about their quality of
life just as importantly

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:30.510
as you do the length of their life.

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:34.290
So hopefully Roxy continues
to be a little rock star

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:35.580
that she is.

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:37.260
She loves her treats.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.570
And I'm really glad that you know,

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:42.998
this terrible situation kind
of had not the worst ending.

00:09:42.998 --> 00:09:46.165
(soft peaceful music)

